# text = She came into the drawing-room at tea-time , but it was evidently an effort to her ; and , as if to make up for some reproachful feeling against her late sister , Miss Jenkyns , which had been troubling her all the afternoon , and for which she now felt penitent , she kept telling me how good and how clever Deborah was in her youth ; how she used to settle what gowns they were to wear at all the parties ( faint , ghostly ideas of grim parties , far away in the distance , when Miss Matty and Miss Pole were young !); and how Deborah and her mother had started the benefit society for the poor , and taught girls cooking and plain sewing ; and how Deborah had once danced with a lord ; and how she used to visit at Sir Peter Arley's , and tried to remodel the quiet rectory establishment on the plans of Arley Hall , where they kept thirty servants ; and how she had nursed Miss Matty through a long , long illness , of which I had never heard before , but which I now dated in my own mind as following the dismissal of the suit of Mr Holbrook .
